WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
INTRO:
Saudi Arabia is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:06.960
universally known for their massive oil reserves&nbsp;
and extreme wealth. Their flagship company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:13.140
Saudi Aramco, is worth over $2 trillion and has&nbsp;
annual earnings exceeding a quarter trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:20.400
dollars. They also apparently have 266 billion&nbsp;
barrels of oil which works out to roughly ⅙ of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.020
the entire world’s reserves. With such large&nbsp;
reserves, you would think that the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.840
would avoid importing oil like the plague, but&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia has actually been doing the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:37.020
opposite. They’ve actually been doubling down&nbsp;
on oil imports since June. And this isn’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:42.540
first time Saudi Arabia has leaned into imports&nbsp;
either. Historically, Saudi Arabia has embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:49.080
oil imports whenever they need more oil. But, why&nbsp;
would a country like Saudi Arabia ever need more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:55.200
oil? Wouldn’t it always just be cheaper to use&nbsp;
their own? I mean just take a look at Venezuela&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.140
who is able to offer subsidized gas for just 8.4&nbsp;
cents per gallon. Saudi Arabia, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:08.160
comes in at $2.34 per gallon which is really not&nbsp;
that impressive for the world’s oil kingdom. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.400
why in the world is Saudi Arabia importing oil and&nbsp;
why are gas prices so high in Saudi Arabia itself?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.300
OIL IMBALANCES:&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:24.540
One of the primary reasons that Saudi Arabia turns&nbsp;
to imports is because of the different types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:31.080
oil. To most of us, all crude oil probably appear&nbsp;
the same, but there’s actually six different types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:36.420
of oil. These oils are usually classified into&nbsp;
three main categories which are light, medium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.700
and heavy. The attribute that differentiates&nbsp;
these three categories is a scale called API&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.220
gravities that essentially ranks these oils based&nbsp;
on their densities in comparison to water. Within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:53.040
these main categories, you have two sub categories&nbsp;
which are sweet and sour. These sub categories are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.780
reflective of the given oil’s sulfur content.&nbsp;
The lower the sulfur content the sweeter the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.700
oil. Technically, we do have processes to&nbsp;
convert between the various types of oil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.860
At the end of the day, it really just comes to&nbsp;
removing impurities or varying the sulfur content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.960
But, more times than not, it’s cheaper to use&nbsp;
these oils in their natural state than trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.240
convert them into a different state. Light crude&nbsp;
oil is generally the most valuable type of oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:24.180
given that it’s the easiest to refine and has the&nbsp;
largest amount of use cases. This is type of oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:30.000
that is used to produce gasoline, diesel, and&nbsp;
aviation fuel. In other words, this is the type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.940
of oil that runs the world. Heavy crude oil, on&nbsp;
the other hand, is also pretty useful given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.800
it’s what’s used in most manufacturing processes.&nbsp;
Heavy crude plays an essential role in producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.140
industrial products such as asphalt and plastics.&nbsp;
With light and heavy out of the way, that leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.360
us with medium crude oil which is usually the&nbsp;
most underappreciated. Medium crude is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.540
the most common type of oil, but there aren’t&nbsp;
very many uses for it in its natural state. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.594
medium crude is usually distilled to create light&nbsp;
crude. With the three main categories out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.594 --> 00:03:03.600
way, we can move onto the subcategories are sweet&nbsp;
and sour. For the most part, sweet crude is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.640
the world craves and sour crude is generally just&nbsp;
converted into sweet crude. With that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.860
the most valuable type of oil has historically&nbsp;
been light sweet crude due to the exponential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.660
growth within the automotive and aviation&nbsp;
industries. But, as the world’s oil consumption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.400
started to stabilize, it became evident that oil&nbsp;
giants may have diverted too much of their focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.840
to light sweet crude. We were starting to get into&nbsp;
a positon where there was an oversupply of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.540
crude and an undersupply of heavy crude. So, while&nbsp;
light sweet has historically demanded a premium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.820
much of that premium has slowly eroded away over&nbsp;
the years, and in some cases, light crude even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:45.180
trades at a discount. I know that all of that info&nbsp;
may have been a little dense, but I think it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.920
important in understanding a country’s complex&nbsp;
oil needs. So, now that we have a basic technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.660
understanding regarding the various types of oil,&nbsp;
let’s delve into why Saudi Arabia imports oil.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.460
SAUDI ARABIAN PRIDE:&nbsp;

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:04.320
Given the success of Saudi Arabia’s oil industry,&nbsp;
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.600
their reserves primarily consist of light crude.&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia does produce five different grades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.280
of oil which are Arabian Heavy, Arabian&nbsp;
Medium, Arabian Light, Arabian Extra Light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.860
and Arabian Super Light. But, as you can see,&nbsp;
three of these are simply sub categories of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:25.560
crude which makes the country prone to imbalances.&nbsp;
Something else to note is that virtually all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.300
Saudi Arabia’s oil reserves are sour oil.&nbsp;
The only exception to this is the offshore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.580
Safaniya oil field. Fortunately, this happens&nbsp;
to be the world’s largest offshore oil field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:41.340
but even Safaniya is only able to produce&nbsp;
roughly 1 million barrels per day. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.740
that’s not a bad amount by any means, but&nbsp;
in comparison to Saudi Arabia’s total oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:54.240
output of 10 million barrels per day, that’s only&nbsp;
10% meaning that 90% of Saudi oil is sour. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.520
while Saudi Arabia has enormous oil reserves,&nbsp;
the vast majority of it is light crude sour oil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:06.000
Naturally, this leads to imbalances which Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia has to make up for using imports. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.800
something that we should note is that Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia is not only looking to make up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:17.460
internal imbalances but international imbalances.&nbsp;
You see, even though Saudi Arabia doesn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.600
the world’s largest oil reserves, they’ve&nbsp;
become the dominant voice within the industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.700
and they’re trusted around the world as the oil&nbsp;
bankers. Saudi Arabia takes this responsibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:34.020
extremely seriously and for them, it has become&nbsp;
a matter of pride and control. Now, this doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.940
mean that if Botswana has a shortage of oil&nbsp;
or if gas prices are skyrocketing in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:44.400
Saudi Arabia is gonna come to the rescue. But,&nbsp;
what this does mean is that they’re willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.200
do whatever it takes to deliver the oil that&nbsp;
they have promised to deliver. And this includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.300
importing oil to make up for supply/production&nbsp;
shortages. A perfect example of this was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:59.640
2019 when their oil processing facilities were&nbsp;
attacked. Drones were used to severely damage oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.640
facilities in Abqaiq and Khurais which disrupted&nbsp;
the vast majority of the country’s oil output. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.440
rebel group from Yemen would claim responsibility,&nbsp;
but given the sophistication of the ordeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:16.680
Saudi Arabia suspects that it was actually&nbsp;
Iran. Iran has profusely refused involvement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:21.480
but really, the identity of the perpetrator&nbsp;
is not really important for what we’re talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.520
about. The point is that Saudi Arabia’s oil&nbsp;
facilities were significantly damaged which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.400
lowered output by 5.7 million barrels per&nbsp;
day. Given the circumstances, I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.200
anyone would have blamed Saudi Arabia if they&nbsp;
fell short of their delivery numbers. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.540
despite this, Saudi Arabia would import oil from&nbsp;
a bunch of foreign oil producers to quote “give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.880
customers every drop of oil they’ve ordered.”&nbsp;
Pride no doubt plays a role in this decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:53.340
but there’s a substantial amount of strategy here&nbsp;
as well. By stepping up to the plate even during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.780
a national crisis, Saudi Arabia has shown the&nbsp;
world that they will stop at nothing to deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:03.300
on their promises. And this has have given the&nbsp;
world very little reason to diversify away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:09.480
Saudi Arabia. Now, imagine if Saudi Arabia didn’t&nbsp;
import oil. Sure, no one would’ve blamed them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.700
but Saudi Arabia’s customers would’ve probably&nbsp;
diversified to other oil producers for their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.160
security. But, by stepping up, Saudi Arabia was&nbsp;
able to maintain the loyalty of their customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.980
and more importantly maintain control, and at&nbsp;
the end of the day, that’s the name of the game.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.000
OPPORTUNIST:&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.520
Keeping control and maintaining pride is not&nbsp;
the only reasons that Saudi Arabia imports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:41.340
oil. Another key reason for Saudi imports is&nbsp;
opportunity and profits, and with the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:48.120
Russia situation going on, this avenue is more&nbsp;
open than ever. By now, I’m sure all of you guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.460
are familiar with the Russian oil sanctions. The&nbsp;
west has decided to boycott Russian products in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:59.460
order to hurt them economically and quote on quote&nbsp;
“teach them a lesson”. And given that Russia was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:03.840
the world’s second largest crude oil exporter&nbsp;
only beaten out by Saudi Arabia themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.360
this has disrupted the global oil supply chain&nbsp;
leading to the super erratic gas prices that we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:14.700
seen over the past several months. While this&nbsp;
is terrible news for westerners, this has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:19.860
a godsend for oil producing countries like Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia who have seen record profits. Higher oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:25.320
prices aren’t the only area in which Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
is profiting though. Even though global oil prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.940
have been skyrocketing, Russian oil prices have&nbsp;
been falling off a cliff given that no western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:35.880
country wants to touch their oil. On average, it&nbsp;
appears that Russia is offering a 30% discount on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:40.380
their oil in comparison to the global benchmark.&nbsp;
If Saudi Arabia really wanted to push the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:46.200
envelope, what they could do is import Russian oil&nbsp;
at a 30% discount and then sell it as their own at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:51.900
market price. But, it’s more than likely that&nbsp;
they’re not doing this. It’s simply not worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:57.480
risking international oil partnerships for an&nbsp;
arbitrage opportunity. But, these rules only apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:03.060
to exports to western countries. Many of Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia’s customers are not western countries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:08.640
they do not care about the source of their oil.&nbsp;
Two of Saudi Arabia’s largest customers are China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:14.880
and India and these guys have actually gone out&nbsp;
of their ways to import Russian oil directly. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:20.040
they definitely do not have a moral dilemma when&nbsp;
it comes Saudi Arabia repackaging Russian oil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:25.800
Someone else who doesn’t have a moral dilemma&nbsp;
with Russian oil is Saudi Arabia themselves. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.240
makes very little sense to use your own oil which&nbsp;
can be sold for top dollar to western countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:37.320
when you can import Russian oil at a 30% discount.&nbsp;
It’s honestly a no brainer and it’s no wonder that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:42.060
Saudi Arabia has been leveraging this strategy.&nbsp;
The real losers in this situation are everyday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:47.040
westerners who are paying top dollar for Middle&nbsp;
Eastern oil. Not to mention, western countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:52.620
are basically still supporting Russia just through&nbsp;
a proxy. This isn’t to say that Russia isn’t being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:57.360
hurt or that the sanctions aren’t effective. I&nbsp;
mean they’re literally having to sell their oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.820
for a 30% discount, so clearly they’re being&nbsp;
hurt. But, the thing note is that western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:08.940
countries aren’t just hurting Russia but they’re&nbsp;
also hurting themselves. And the ones that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:14.640
truly benefitting are the countries in the middle&nbsp;
like China, India, and of course Saudi Arabia.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:20.940
SAUDI IMPORTS EXPLAINED:
In the end, while Saudi oil imports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:25.560
may seem confusing at first glance given their&nbsp;
massive oil reserves, they actually make a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:30.000
of sense when you take a closer look. First of&nbsp;
all, there’s several different types of oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.360
and Saudi Arabia primarily only has light crude&nbsp;
sour oil. So, they often have to import oil to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:41.160
make up for internal imbalances. But, it’s not&nbsp;
just internal imbalances that they’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.780
to make up for. All of Saudi Arabia’s power&nbsp;
originates from their dominance on the global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:51.600
oil markets. If they lose this dominance, they&nbsp;
also lose their power and influence on the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.360
stage which is obviously not something that&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia wants to do. To keep their power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.500
Saudi Arabia is willing to go to extreme&nbsp;
lengths to meet their production numbers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:06.960
deliver to their customers. And yes, this includes&nbsp;
importing oil at a loss to make up for production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:13.500
shortfalls. Imported oil doesn’t always have to&nbsp;
be expensive or undesirable though. Russian oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:19.260
is extremely desirable right now given that it’s&nbsp;
selling for a 30% discount. While Saudi Arabia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:24.360
can’t just turn around and sell this same oil to&nbsp;
western countries, they can use it themselves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:28.860
sell it to neutral countries. This way, they&nbsp;
can save all of their own oil production for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:33.780
western markets where it can be sold for absurd&nbsp;
profit margins, allowing Saudi Arabia to profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:40.920
$1 billion on a daily basis. And that’s why Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia is importing record amounts of oil. Would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.660
you be willing to arbitrage between Russia&nbsp;
and the West? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:49.860
drop a like if you’re hoping that these oil&nbsp;
games come to a close sooner rather than later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

